        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>One dot per state, sorted low to high. The All-India rule (67 per cent) sits in the middle of the strip — meaning half the states are worse than the national average. The spread is enormous: from Kerala's 39 per cent (comparable to middle-income countries) to Ladakh's 92 per cent (nine in ten children).</p>
            <p>The geography confounds income. Gujarat — among India's richest states — has the second-worst child anaemia rate (80 per cent). The north-eastern states cluster better (40s), defying their lower per-capita income. Anaemia follows diet, public health and genetics, not GDP.</p>
