  <!-- § 12 how to misread -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">12</span><h2>How to misread this number</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Anaemia rose from 58% to 67% — children's health is getting worse."</h3>
          <p>It may be — but the survey changed its haemoglobin analyser between rounds (HemoCue Hb 201+ to Hb 301), and the newer device reads higher. Part of the rise is methodological. The uniform direction across every group (children, women, men) signals a real component, but the survey report itself cautions against taking the full increase at face value.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"TFR is below replacement — India doesn't need population control."</h3>
          <p>National TFR is 1.99, below the replacement rate of 2.1. But four large states — Bihar, Uttar Pradesh, Jharkhand, Meghalaya — are still above it. India's population will keep growing for decades because of momentum (a large youth cohort entering reproductive age), even with below-replacement fertility. "Below replacement" does not mean "shrinking."</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Institutional births reached 89% — maternal health is solved."</h3>
          <p>Institutional delivery measures where the birth happened, not what happened there. A woman who delivers in a facility but receives no postnatal check-up, or has a C-section without medical indication, is counted the same as one with full care. The rise in institutional births is a supply-side achievement; the quality of care inside the facility is a different question.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iv.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Women's bank accounts jumped from 3% to 79% — women are financially empowered."</h3>
          <p>The accounts exist — the Jan Dhan Yojana opened them. Whether women control them is another matter. Many are DBT conduits (benefits transferred in, withdrawn by a male relative); the survey counts account ownership, not account use. The rise is real; financial empowerment is the step after.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">v.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Gujarat has 80% child anaemia — it's a poor state."</h3>
          <p>Gujarat is among India's richest states by per-capita income. Anaemia does not follow income: it follows diet (iron, folate, B12 intake), infection load (malaria, hookworm), genetics (thalassaemia, sickle-cell trait) and food systems. Wealth buys treatment, not prevention; the anaemia geography confounds the income geography.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">vi.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"These two rounds show a trend."</h3>
          <p>They show a comparison. Two points do not make a trend; they make a direction. The next round (NFHS-6, expected 2024-25) will confirm whether anaemia's rise is a trend or a methodological artefact, whether fertility keeps falling, whether the empowerment gains hold. Until then, read the arrows as one interval's movement, not a trajectory.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

